## Finding a Suitable Confidence Threshold

We need to find a confidence threshold where detections below this cutoff is excluded. This is a precision-recall tradeoff because a   
high confidence threshold like 0.75 could mean fewer false positives but we miss real vehicle detections, which ultimately results in   
undercounting. Meanwhile, a very low threshold (e.g., 0.10 - 0.15) will have the detector spotting almost every vehicle, but it can also   
start hallucinating, boxing spots that are not objects at all. This risks creating phantom counts and tracks since the tracker tries to   
assign IDs to these detections. The goal is finding the sweet spot where hallucinations are gone but real vehicles still survive. 

We want to know:

- At what confidence do false positives start appearing?
- At what confidence do real vehicles start getting cut?

Note that "false positive" here is relative to the task, not the model. The model can correctly detect non-vehicle objects like people,   
unless we later tune it to detect only vehicle types. A person or a fire hydrant detected at high confidence isn’t a hallucination. 
The model genuinely sees an object there. Those can be handled separately by class filtering, not by the confidence threshold.  

